course: dictionary
lesson: 13609
title: Incurable
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Incurable is built from three parts: in- means not, cure means to make well, and -able means can be. So an incurable illness is one that cannot be made well. «The doctor said the illness was incurable, so she could not give Sam a medicine to make it go away.» Doctors can still help a person feel better.
explain: The word can also describe a habit or a character. «Grandad is an incurable joker.» Nobody can cure a joking habit, so it means his jokes will never stop. People often say this with a smile, to show that someone is always like that.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. If the sentence says the illness cannot go away, or the habit will never change, incurable is probably right. Check by putting "cannot be cured" in place of the word and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Incurable comes from in- (not), cure (to make well) and -able (can be).
fact: «An incurable disease» means a disease that cannot be cured.
fact: «Incurable» can also describe a strong habit, as in «an incurable optimist», someone who always hopes for the best.
fact: The adverb is «incurably»: «He was incurably curious about how things worked.»
fact: Doctors can still care for a person with an incurable illness, helping them feel better.
exercise: «The illness is incurable.» What does incurable mean here? | Can be cured in one day | Very loud and noisy | Lost for ever in the woods | *Cannot be cured
exercise: «Ben is an incurable chatterbox.» What is Ben like? | He is ill at home | Very quiet and shy | A very fast runner | *He will never stop talking
exercise: The doctor says the illness is incurable. What can the doctor still do? | Send the illness away for ever | Cure it by lunchtime | *Help the person feel better | Make the illness disappear with one pill
test: What does «an incurable romantic» mean? | *Someone who will never stop being romantic | Someone who is sick in bed | Someone who is rich | Someone who has lost their heart
test: Which part of incurable means not? | *in- | cure | -able | incur
exam: Jamal says, «The illness is incurable, so it will go away next week.» Is he right? | Yes, incurable means it goes away quickly | Yes, because doctors always cure everything | No, incurable means it is very loud | *No, incurable means it cannot be cured
